{
  "requirement": "The method is declared under {cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.check_inputs`.\n\nThe method resolves the recorded dependencies through graph operations and\nfingerprinting, comparing each with its saved observation. A fingerprint\nmismatch returns `Ok(false)` and permits an early return; it need not resolve\nlater dependencies. An encountered dependency-resolution failure returns an\nerror.\n\nThe method observes the current state that the caller intends to inspect.\nFreshness checking has a separate observation scope from criterion execution.\nChecked observations enter the new trace when execution explicitly reads\nor delivers them.\nA checked snapshot can be reused if it still represents the current state.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests cover each testable obligation of `requirements.sdk.graph.evidence.checking_records`. I did not request implementation source, because the tests' assertions were enough to map each obligation.\n\n- **Compare each dependency with its saved observation; a mismatch returns `Ok(false)`.**\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[a_matching_dependency_does_not_hide_a_later_changed_dependency]` covers both `Dependency::Node` and `Dependency::OutgoingEdges`. A matching first dependency still gives a stale result when a later one changes.\n  - `fn[wildcard_versions_still_check_inputs_and_errors]` covers a changed content fingerprint and a fingerprint with a different scheme. Both give a non-current result.\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[changing_one_criteria_input_does_not_invalidate_another]` calls `check_inputs` directly. It asserts `false` for the changed record and `true` for the unchanged one.\n  - `fn[absence_certification_is_invalidated_by_the_first_selected_root]` asserts `false` for a changed edge-selection root and `true` for an unchanged one.\n  - `fn[dependency_resolution_failures_do_not_execute_or_replace_saved_answers]` asserts `Ok(true)`, then `Ok(false)` after the edges change.\n  - `fn[labeled_selections_track_only_target_membership_without_reading_contents]` checks edge-membership freshness through the graph-operation path. It also asserts that no content reads happen.\n  - `src/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[requirement_edges_and_input_freshness_use_the_sdk]` checks fresh and stale outcomes on the real domain.\n- **An encountered resolution failure returns an error.**\n  - `fn[wildcard_versions_still_check_inputs_and_errors]` asserts `Err(\"read\")`.\n  - `fn[labeled_selections_track_only_target_membership_without_reading_contents]` asserts `Err(\"edges\")`.\n  - `fn[dependency_resolution_failures_do_not_execute_or_replace_saved_answers]` asserts both `Err(\"read\")` and `Err(\"edges\")` through `check_inputs`. The edges failure is the second dependency, after a matching `Node` dependency. The test also asserts that no new execution or write occurs.\n- **Early return on mismatch.** The text only says the method \"need not\" resolve later dependencies. That is permission, so it imposes nothing to test.\n- **Freshness checking has a separate observation scope from execution.**\n  - `fn[historical_freshness_reads_do_not_enter_the_replacement_trace]` reads node 2 during the check. The new record's dependencies contain only node 1. The read order is `[2, 1]`.\n  - `fn[input_checking_and_execution_keep_separate_observations]` shows that a checked read does not leak into the new trace. When execution explicitly reads node 2, it records its own fresh fingerprint (\"after\").\n  - `fn[typed_loading_reports_decode_errors_even_for_stale_evidence]` and `fn[refresh_evaluates_missing_incompatible_or_changed_evidence_with_a_fresh_trace]` show that no dependency resolution happens during a new execution and that the replacement trace is fresh. The refresh test's per-cause read order, `[2, 1]` for changed contents, confirms the check observation stays separate.\n- **Reuse of a checked snapshot.** The text says it \"can\" be reused. That is permissive and needs no test.\n- **Declared under `check_inputs`.** The `check_inputs` tests above call `Validator::check_inputs` directly.\n\nI found no plausible requirement-violating behavior that these tests would accept.",
